rname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({225}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265794 13194139533918 true] [17592186265793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265795 13194139533918 true] [17592186265793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265796 13194139533918 true] [17592186265793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265797 13194139533918 true] [17592186265793 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265798 13194139533918 true] [17592186265793 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533918 true] [17592186265793 1041 "selection" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265793 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265795 13194139533918 true] [17592186265793 1110 #uuid "f37d886e-a3db-54ed-8136-8135816a8801" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265794 1034 "(-2,-\\sqrt{2})" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265794 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265794 1111 #uuid "c6fbc8ef-f30c-5f1e-b016-24b45c931a18" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265795 1034 "(-2\\sqrt{2},-2\\sqrt{2})" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265795 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265795 1111 #uuid "572c37de-c6e1-5111-ae49-cd7109e007da" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265796 1034 "(2\\sqrt{2},-2)" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265796 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265796 1111 #uuid "2c07b934-6c82-5ff9-8e4e-c582f03b2d7b" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265797 1034 "(2\\sqrt{2},-1)" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265797 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265797 1111 #uuid "8fa31c6e-d533-5d17-8e4e-2327ffacc325" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265798 1034 "(-2,2)" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265798 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265798 1111 #uuid "ea306d4e-4767-5838-a873-081559cb2fc9" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265799 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265800 13194139533918 true] [17592186265799 1089 "q-120328" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265799 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533918 true] [17592186265799 1100 "Given that the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $6,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${315}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, what are the Cartesian coordinates of $P?$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265799 1113 #uuid "dd295ef1-565e-5082-b9b4-b1e2a3d77e84" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265799 1145 "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=6,\\,\\theta ={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =6\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =6(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265801 13194139533918 true] [17592186265800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265802 13194139533918 true] [17592186265800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265803 13194139533918 true] [17592186265800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265804 13194139533918 true] [17592186265800 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265805 13194139533918 true] [17592186265800 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533918 true] [17592186265800 1041 "selection" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265800 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265804 13194139533918 true] [17592186265800 1110 #uuid "3eb2da5f-b177-521a-89fc-c3bd4625394a" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265801 1034 "(\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2},-\\frac{3\\sqrt{2}}{2})" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265801 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265801 1111 #uuid "597879b4-34e1-5300-acf7-fc9ab54b42ff" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265802 1034 "(2\\sqrt{3},-2\\sqrt{3})" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265802 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265802 1111 #uuid "c26a53a0-91c4-52d4-baab-d09ef8fabb4d" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265803 1034 "(\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3},-\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3})" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265803 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265803 1111 #uuid "9926c3e4-724d-5bf3-b4f6-ba23068c972c" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265804 1034 "(3\\sqrt{2},-3\\sqrt{2})" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265804 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265804 1111 #uuid "723bc5ed-fa57-5534-be9f-fb42a5237071" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265805 1034 "(-6\\sqrt{2},6\\sqrt{2})" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265805 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265805 1111 #uuid "438f43d4-5f45-5c1a-b6af-5f42ad3c058b" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265806 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265807 13194139533918 true] [17592186265806 1089 "q-120468" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265806 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533918 true] [17592186265806 1100 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(2,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{3}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(-\\sqrt{3},-1).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265806 1113 #uuid "256dfcfa-6ea4-5478-bf71-0a81ef7db2c2" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265806 1145 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{-1}{-\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(1/\\sqrt{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the third quadrant, we have $\\theta =\\pi +{\\theta }_{R}=\\frac{7\\pi }{6}.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(4,\\frac{7\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{3})=(4,\\frac{3\\pi }{2}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{3\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(0,-4).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265807 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265808 13194139533918 true] [17592186265807 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265809 13194139533918 true] [17592186265807 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265810 13194139533918 true] [17592186265807 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265811 13194139533918 true] [17592186265807 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265812 13194139533918 true] [17592186265807 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533918 true] [17592186265807 1041 "selection" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265807 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265811 13194139533918 true] [17592186265807 1110 #uuid "2def96d6-5ad6-5ae8-a97f-084f0839a124" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265808 1034 "(2\\sqrt{3},-2)" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265808 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265808 1111 #uuid "b0599bbf-0a2a-5bf6-acdd-acbe5d243900" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265809 1034 "(1,3)" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265809 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265809 1111 #uuid "bc77e445-0135-5876-a0a7-dec8d36ff865" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265810 1034 "(-2,-2\\sqrt{3})" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265810 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265810 1111 #uuid "b57ebf74-1109-5e2c-aced-3cda5c4c6308" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265811 1034 "(0,-4)" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265811 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265811 1111 #uuid "66c4b605-0aca-51e3-b8fe-e6c77be3a459" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265812 1034 "(-4,0)" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265812 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265812 1111 #uuid "98ab9bcc-c3cf-50a5-8d34-6919868f579e" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265813 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265814 13194139533918 true] [17592186265813 1089 "q-120595" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265813 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533918 true] [17592186265813 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/5ff0219f3f76441dbffc770ce0b8f2481cca8c46b8aa832ab7893d472f3026ae.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6})$ and $m∠AOB=\\frac{\\pi }{6},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265813 1113 #uuid "de8834e5-df0a-5c36-914b-a1ed971ccd20" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265813 1145 "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,\\frac{5\\pi }{6}),$ we must have $r=4.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is $\\frac{5\\pi }{6},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}-\\frac{\\pi }{6}=\\frac{2\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4,\\frac{2\\pi }{3})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{2\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-2,2\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265814 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265815 13194139533918 true] [17592186265814 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265816 13194139533918 true] [17592186265814 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265817 13194139533918 true] [17592186265814 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265818 13194139533918 true] [17592186265814 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265819 13194139533918 true] [17592186265814 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533918 true] [17592186265814 1041 "selection" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265814 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265815 13194139533918 true] [17592186265814 1110 #uuid "aee33c74-a634-5edd-9349-06ba0d8bbd43" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265815 1034 "(-2,2\\sqrt{3})" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265815 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265815 1111 #uuid "0713e35e-6435-5ac5-b404-deb8a7f6b610" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265816 1034 "(2\\sqrt{3},-2)" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265816 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265816 1111 #uuid "b5a250a8-3dfa-5f81-8455-561a61b5b21a" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265817 1034 "(2\\sqrt{3},2\\sqrt{3})" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265817 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265817 1111 #uuid "993e94ea-20f3-5b92-bfb2-0985049bca80" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265818 1034 "(-2\\sqrt{3},2)" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265818 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265818 1111 #uuid "2b957cb0-8603-5450-8ba2-baad7c038789" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265819 1034 "(-2,2)" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265819 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265819 1111 #uuid "777a4b0d-d347-581d-879a-af2dca7cbe74" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265820 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265821 13194139533918 true] [17592186265820 1089 "q-120597" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265820 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533918 true] [17592186265820 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/fd38e4bed4f11f07f2c6901a74c7543655f5ff8a95b8b049e54ff4ff4dd409ee.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. If the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={165}^{∘},$ what are the Cartesian coordinates of $B?$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265820 1113 #uuid "ae9fb0df-51c8-5805-8a9a-3fb0f6d09879" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265820 1145 "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4\\sqrt{2},{150}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=4\\sqrt{2}.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4\\sqrt{2},$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4\\sqrt{2}.$  \n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counterclockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${150}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={150}^{∘}+{165}^{∘}={315}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},{315}^{∘})$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(4,-4).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265822 13194139533918 true] [17592186265821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265823 13194139533918 true] [17592186265821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265824 13194139533918 true] [17592186265821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265825 13194139533918 true] [17592186265821 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265826 13194139533918 true] [17592186265821 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139533918 true] [17592186265821 1041 "selection" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265821 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265825 13194139533918 true] [17592186265821 1110 #uuid "ce045a4f-b2e7-5441-89c8-84712af99447" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265822 1034 "(-4\\sqrt{2},-4)" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265822 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265822 1111 #uuid "59089e7d-733a-5a7d-9851-d20e38c5e458" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265823 1034 "(2,-2\\sqrt{2})" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265823 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265823 1111 #uuid "153bf9d5-5ec5-55ff-96e2-6a413e26cff2" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265824 1034 "(-2,-2)" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265824 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265824 1111 #uuid "0e2ab7a2-ece3-55d4-a636-667e4ec5fcf9" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265825 1034 "(4,-4)" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265825 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265825 1111 #uuid "e2b14ad3-33d1-50ce-9cd6-5969e82aba55" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265826 1034 "(4,4\\sqrt{2})" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265826 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265826 1111 #uuid "d9ce342e-755b-59