$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =8\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{7\\pi }{4}) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) \\\\  & =-4\\sqrt{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(4\\sqrt{2},-4\\sqrt{2}).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265858 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265859 13194139533918 true] [17592186265858 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533918 true] [17592186265858 1041 "field-1" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265858 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265859 13194139533918 true] [17592186265858 1110 #uuid "8a0f9566-5e77-5620-9dd0-3bfc1a02b3de" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265859 1034 "4\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265859 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265859 1111 #uuid "3b3acb1a-ab0a-5b22-9b41-323026d642fb" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265860 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265861 13194139533918 true] [17592186265860 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533918 true] [17592186265860 1041 "field-2" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265860 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265861 13194139533918 true] [17592186265860 1110 #uuid "a5443125-7f8c-5e98-af18-c55fd5aeeb59" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265861 1034 "-4\\sqrt{2}" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265861 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265861 1111 #uuid "7ac96947-efdd-550e-a44e-41f65188f306" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265862 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265863 13194139533918 true] [17592186265862 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265865 13194139533918 true] [17592186265862 1089 "q-276791" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265862 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533918 true] [17592186265862 1100 "If the distance from a point $P$ to the origin is $12,$ and that $P$ makes an angle of ${60}^{∘}$ with the positive $x$-axis, then the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are$\\,(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265862 1113 #uuid "91859d58-dbae-5830-8515-ca5a18c13524" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265862 1145 "We notice that the point $P$ is described using polar coordinates $(r,\\theta )$, where\n\n$$\nr=12,\\,\\theta ={60}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nTo convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $P:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =12\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡({60}^{∘}) \\\\  & =12\\cdot (\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =6\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $P$ are $(6,6\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265863 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265864 13194139533918 true] [17592186265863 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533918 true] [17592186265863 1041 "field-1" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265863 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265864 13194139533918 true] [17592186265863 1110 #uuid "9fd9d0c0-f207-55c3-be9d-9b5a92b66369" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265864 1034 "6" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265864 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265864 1111 #uuid "057b6b8e-7efa-5e4a-9f17-42680386a2ba" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265865 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265866 13194139533918 true] [17592186265865 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533918 true] [17592186265865 1041 "field-2" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265865 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265866 13194139533918 true] [17592186265865 1110 #uuid "f64753f3-c12f-53f3-a5a1-c80e9f76c8c7" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265866 1034 "6\\sqrt{3}" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265866 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265866 1111 #uuid "3c6418e2-73ac-5e70-89f7-cc821a004a8f" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265867 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265868 13194139533918 true] [17592186265867 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265870 13194139533918 true] [17592186265867 1089 "q-276803" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265867 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533918 true] [17592186265867 1100 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(r,\\frac{\\pi }{6})$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(\\frac{r}{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$ Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(\\sqrt{3},1).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265867 1113 #uuid "188fe0f5-6988-52fa-91db-08219b61d9bf" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265867 1145 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find $r,$ the distance between $A$ and the origin:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r & =\\sqrt{{x}^{2}+{y}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{{(\\sqrt{3})}^{2}+{1}^{2}} \\\\  & =\\sqrt{4} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{2}{3},\\frac{5\\pi }{6}).$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{3}(\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3},\\frac{1}{3}).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265868 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265869 13194139533918 true] [17592186265868 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533918 true] [17592186265868 1041 "field-1" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265868 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265869 13194139533918 true] [17592186265868 1110 #uuid "0c14cda7-3017-549b-a975-ccfa2bad06de" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265869 1034 "-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{3}" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265869 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265869 1111 #uuid "dcd50afe-3eb7-5386-b4d6-52582e3a30cd" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265870 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265871 13194139533918 true] [17592186265870 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533918 true] [17592186265870 1041 "field-2" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265870 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265871 13194139533918 true] [17592186265870 1110 #uuid "4c9310a9-02c3-566b-aee6-6d2953623dfd" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265871 1034 "\\frac{1}{3}" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265871 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265871 1111 #uuid "0eebaf68-1aa6-5758-a417-bf5386698813" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265872 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265873 13194139533918 true] [17592186265872 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265875 13194139533918 true] [17592186265872 1089 "q-276838" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265872 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139533918 true] [17592186265872 1100 "The point $A$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\theta )$ and the point $B$ has polar coordinates $(5,\\theta +\\frac{\\pi }{6}).$ Find the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ are $(5,0).$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265872 1113 #uuid "f9180b14-eb26-561a-9fe7-e0f704e71580" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265872 1145 "First, we use the Cartesian coordinates of $A$ to find its reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\theta }_{R} & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{0}{5}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(0) \\\\  & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the point $A$ lies in the first quadrant, we have $\\theta ={\\theta }_{R}=0.$ Consequently, the polar coordinates of $B$ are\n\n$$\n(5,0+\\frac{\\pi }{6})=(5,\\frac{\\pi }{6}).\n$$\n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{6}) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{2}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2},\\frac{5}{2}).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265873 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265874 13194139533918 true] [17592186265873 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533918 true] [17592186265873 1041 "field-1" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265873 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265874 13194139533918 true] [17592186265873 1110 #uuid "8a9ad845-ec06-5c93-a188-15ae28b31b4a" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265874 1034 "\\frac{5\\sqrt{3}}{2}" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265874 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265874 1111 #uuid "5c40f843-0631-58b4-a06f-6194eb70da85" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265875 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265876 13194139533918 true] [17592186265875 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533918 true] [17592186265875 1041 "field-2" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265875 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265876 13194139533918 true] [17592186265875 1110 #uuid "13d93c35-a9f4-5a65-afe7-4b3454045b6d" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265876 1034 "\\frac{5}{2}" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265876 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265876 1111 #uuid "b8753972-949b-559c-ae7f-2d99a21abb54" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265877 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265878 13194139533918 true] [17592186265877 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265880 13194139533918 true] [17592186265877 1089 "q-277012" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265877 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533918 true] [17592186265877 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/d931b377a8d62bf3b56672a0f556c0581e8400c5b1c7b1f783f08e4297342855.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(4,{240}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={210}^{∘}.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265877 1113 #uuid "a83f5e13-021d-5699-bc54-d8ad3c050b9c" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265877 1145 "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(4,{240}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=4.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $4,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $4.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${240}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={240}^{∘}-{210}^{∘}={30}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(4,{30}^{∘})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{cos}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =2\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =4\\operatorname{sin}⁡({30}^{∘}) \\\\  & =4\\cdot \\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =2\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(2\\sqrt{3},2).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265878 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265879 13194139533918 true] [17592186265878 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533918 true] [17592186265878 1041 "field-1" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265878 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265879 13194139533918 true] [17592186265878 1110 #uuid "7f71cd2d-b7fb-5953-aada-7492f4f7add2" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265879 1034 "2\\sqrt{3}" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265879 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265879 1111 #uuid "c59c4311-7e00-5f7f-8733-9fa2f55748aa" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265880 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265881 13194139533918 true] [17592186265880 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533918 true] [17592186265880 1041 "field-2" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265880 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265881 13194139533918 true] [17592186265880 1110 #uuid "0f039044-546d-51dc-a1c5-66b80a481b23" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265881 1034 "2" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265881 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265881 1111 #uuid "967cbe30-b750-5581-a87f-808a1ee4c15d" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265882 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265883 13194139533918 true] [17592186265882 1017 #edb/ref 17592186265885 13194139533918 true] [17592186265882 1089 "q-277027" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265882 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045464 13194139533918 true] [17592186265882 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13952748/assets/dbca0b9bd96eb68b7f874df74848bb887037a7de2ed7613bc848c40f78feff4f.png)\n\nThe diagram above shows a circle of radius $r$ centered at the origin. Determine the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ given that the polar coordinates of the point $A$ are $(10,{60}^{∘})$ and $m∠AOB={60}^{∘}.$\n\nThe Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(${{field-1}}$,$\n{{field-2}}$).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265882 1113 #uuid "01215fdb-7672-5686-a3c5-4762cb00c7f3" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265882 1145 "First, since $A$ has polar coordinates $(10,{60}^{∘}),$ we must have $r=10.$ Also, since the points $A$ and $B$ both lie on a circle of radius $10,$ the $r$-coordinate of $B$ must also be $10.$\n\nNotice that one must traverse the circle counter-clockwise to move from the point $A$ to point $B.$ Since the $\\theta $-coordinate of $A$ is ${60}^{∘},$ the $\\theta $-coordinate of $B$ must be\n\n$$\n{\\theta }_{B}={60}^{∘}+{60}^{∘}={120}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nSo, the polar coordinates of $B$ are $(10,{120}^{∘})$ \n\nNow, we convert $B$ to Cartesian coordinates. To convert from polar coordinates to Cartesian coordinates, we use\n\n$$\nx=r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,y=r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta .\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the $x$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & =r\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{cos}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{2}) \\\\  & =-5\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we calculate the $y$-coordinate of $B:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =r\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta  \\\\  & =10\\operatorname{sin}⁡({120}^{∘}) \\\\  & =10\\cdot \\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} \\\\  & =5\\sqrt{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Cartesian coordinates of $B$ are $(-5,5\\sqrt{3}).$" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265883 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265884 13194139533918 true] [17592186265883 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045449 13194139533918 true] [17592186265883 1041 "field-1" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265883 1104 #edb/ref 17592186265884 13194139533918 true] [17592186265883 1110 #uuid "2e6b5ca2-baf7-510e-8c4f-1ee7f6e5f63b" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265884 1034 "-5" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265884 1103 #edb/ref 17592186045453 13194139533918 true] [17592186265884 1111 #uuid "caf700d8-e674-5c42-bdca-25ab22c82965" 13194139533918 true] [17592186265885 1000 #edb/ref 17592186265886 13194139533918 true] [17592186265885 1040 #edb/ref 17592